## The Borrow Checker's Rules

The borrow checker enforces three core rules at compile time. Understanding these rules eliminates whole classes of memory bugs.

### The Three Rules

1. **Many shared borrows** (`&T`) or **one mutable borrow** (`&mut T`), never both
2. **Borrows must not outlive** the owner
3. **References must always be valid** — no dangling pointers

### Rule 1: Shared XOR Mutable

```xiom
var data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1);

// VALID: multiple shared borrows
let r1 = &data;
let r2 = &data;
io.println(r1.len());
io.println(r2.len());

// VALID: single mutable borrow
let m = &mut data;
m.push(2);

// INVALID: shared borrow while mutable exists
// let r = &data;  // if m is still alive

// INVALID: two mutable borrows
// let m1 = &mut data;
// let m2 = &mut data;
```

### Rule 2: Lifetime — Borrow Must Not Outlive Owner

```xiom
// INVALID: returning reference to local variable
// fn dangle() -> &Vec[Int] {
//   let v = Vec[Int]();
//   return &v;  // v destroyed at end of function!
// }
```

### Rule 3: References Always Valid

```xiom
// INVALID: reference to moved value
// let v = Vec[Int]();
// v.push(1);
// let r = &v;
// let v2 = v;  // MOVE — invalidates r
// io.println(r.len());  // ERROR: r references moved value
```

### Working With the Rules

```xiom
// Narrow borrow scopes with blocks
var data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1);
{
  let m = &mut data;
  m.push(2);
}  // borrow ends here
let r = &data;  // now we can shared-borrow
let len = r.len();

// Clone to break borrow conflicts
let copy = data.clone();  // independent copy
// data and copy can be borrowed independently
```

### Why These Rules?

They prevent data races, use-after-free, double-free, and iterator invalidation — all at compile time, with zero runtime cost.

